// Deck-based explanations and original exercises. $...$ is inline mathematics.
export const equationContext={};
const F=(title,math,note,purpose,read,symbols,example,insight)=>{equationContext[title]={purpose,read,symbols,example,insight};return [title,math,note]};
export const lessons=[
{title:'Learn patterns that travel',short:'Generalization and capacity',slides:[2,3,4,15,16,17,18,19],time:'10 min',lead:'A model succeeds when it predicts new examples well, not when it merely remembers the training set.',
idea:'Generalization means carrying what was learned to unseen examples from the relevant distribution. Overfitting occurs when training captures accidental details of the sample, including noise, at the expense of this ability. Capacity is the range of functions a model can represent. Too little capacity can underfit; high capacity can fit the training set extremely well while generalizing poorly. Regularization introduces preferences or constraints that can improve this tradeoff.',
formulas:[],steps:['Separate training data, validation data used for selection, and test data reserved for final evaluation.','Compare training and validation error together: high errors on both suggest underfitting; low training error with much larger validation error suggests overfitting.','Read the polynomial examples: a line misses the quadratic trend; a flexible sixth-degree curve can chase noise; the quadratic captures the intended signal.'],
caveat:'Slides 2 and 15–19 use test performance to explain generalization conceptually. During development, use validation data for choosing models and regularizers; repeated choices based on test results leak information. Capacity alone does not determine overfitting: sample size, noise, optimization and regularization also matter.',
check:'Training error falls from 8 to 2, while validation error rises from 9 to 14. Is training necessarily improving the model?',answer:'It is improving fit to the training set while validation performance worsens. This pattern suggests overfitting; investigate regularization and checkpoint selection rather than assuming lower training error is always better.',figure:18,caption:'The flexible polynomial follows training details but misses unseen observations. Compare the underfit and appropriate-fit examples on slides 17 and 19.'},
{title:'Charge the model for large weights',short:'L2 and L1 penalties',slides:[5,46],time:'12 min',lead:'A weight penalty changes what counts as a good solution: fit the data while avoiding an unnecessarily costly parameter vector.',
idea:'A penalized objective adds a parameter cost to the data-fitting loss. L2 squares the penalized weights, so large magnitudes become especially expensive. Its gradient pulls weights toward zero. L1 uses absolute values and can encourage exact zeros under suitable optimization, yielding sparse solutions. Stronger penalties can reduce overfitting, but can also prevent the model from fitting a real pattern.',
formulas:[F('Squared loss with L2 penalty',String.raw`J(\theta)=\frac1N\sum_{n=1}^{N}(y_n-f(x_n;\theta))^2+\lambda\theta^T\theta`, 'Slide 5. No factor of 1/2 is included in this convention.',
'A training loss alone rewards any parameter vector that fits the examples. The added penalty expresses a preference for smaller weights.', 'Average the squared prediction errors, then add lambda times the sum of squared weights.', [['$J$ and $N$','The full training objective and the number of examples.'],[String.raw`$y_n$ and $f(x_n;\theta)$`,'The target and prediction for example n.'],[String.raw`$\theta^T\theta=\sum_j\theta_j^2$`,'Transpose the weight vector and multiply it by itself: sum its squared entries.'],[String.raw`$\lambda\ge0$`,'Regularization strength. Larger values give the penalty more influence.']],
String.raw`If the average squared error is 2, the weights are $(1,-2)$, and $\lambda=1/10$, the penalty is $(1/10)(1+4)=1/2$ and $J=5/2$.`, String.raw`For this convention the penalty gradient is $2\lambda\theta$. Whether biases are penalized is an implementation choice; often only selected weights are.`),
F('L1 versus L2 · supplementary detail',String.raw`\Omega_{L1}(\theta)=\lambda\sum_j|\theta_j|,\qquad \nabla_\theta\Omega_{L2}=2\lambda\theta`, 'L1 is named in slide 46; its explicit formula and gradient comparison are supplements.',
'Different penalties express different preferences: small magnitudes or sparse parameters.', 'L1 adds absolute weight magnitudes. L2 supplies a pull proportional to each weight.', [[String.raw`$|\theta_j|$`,'Absolute value: distance of weight j from zero.'],[String.raw`$\Omega$`,'The penalty part of the objective, without the data loss.'],[String.raw`$\nabla_\theta$`,'The vector of partial derivatives with respect to the weights.'],[String.raw`$\lambda$`,'The nonnegative strength of the penalty.']],
String.raw`For weights $(1,-2)$ and $\lambda=1$, L1 costs 3 and L2 costs 5. L2’s gradient is $(2,-4)$.`, 'The cost values do not determine which regularizer is best. L1 has a corner at zero, where an ordinary derivative is not defined; appropriate subgradient or proximal methods handle it.')],
steps:['Write the data loss and penalty separately before adding them.','Check whether the exercise averages or sums losses and includes a factor of 1/2; these change the strength convention.','Tune the penalty on validation data and check for underfitting as well as overfitting.'],caveat:'L2 penalization and weight decay are equivalent under a basic SGD update with the matching convention, but that equivalence does not generally hold for adaptive optimizers such as Adam. Feature scales also affect how a coefficient penalty behaves.',check:'Why can increasing the penalty make both training and validation performance worse?',answer:'Excessive shrinkage can stop the model from representing the genuine signal. Regularization is a tradeoff, not a guarantee that stronger is better.',figure:5,caption:'Separate the average fitting error from the weight penalty. The weight penalty is not a penalty on the prediction error itself.'},
{title:'Turn probability into a training loss',short:'Likelihood, IID and squared loss',slides:[6,7,8,9,10,11,12],time:'14 min',lead:'Maximum likelihood asks which parameter values make the observed targets most plausible under the assumed model.',
idea:'Likelihood treats the observed data as fixed and compares possible parameter settings. Under conditional independence, per-example target likelihoods multiply. Taking a logarithm turns that product into a sum; negating it turns maximization into minimization. For a linear prediction with independent Gaussian residual noise of fixed variance, the negative log-likelihood has a squared-error term plus a term constant with respect to the weights.',
formulas:[F('Likelihood and negative log-likelihood',String.raw`p(Y\mid X,\theta)=\prod_{n=1}^N p(y_n\mid x_n,\theta),\qquad\mathcal L(\theta)=-\sum_{n=1}^N\log p(y_n\mid x_n,\theta)`, 'Slides 7–11. The targets are observations; notation is normalized to y rather than the deck’s ŷ.',
String.raw`Multiplying many small probabilities can give a number so close to zero that a computer rounds it to zero. Slide 10 uses $\log(ab)=\log a+\log b$ so we can take each example’s log first and then add. A larger likelihood has a larger log and therefore a smaller negative log, so minimizing this loss picks the same weights as maximizing likelihood.`,
'For each example, take the log of its likelihood and flip the sign. Add those contributions to get the training loss.', [['$X,Y$','All observed inputs and targets; n indexes one paired example.'],[String.raw`$p(y_n\mid x_n,\theta)$`,'Probability mass, or density for a continuous target, under the model. Densities can exceed 1.'],[String.raw`$\prod$ and $\sum$`,'Product and sum, respectively.'],[String.raw`$\mathcal L$ and $\log$`,'Negative log-likelihood and logarithm. Use a consistent log base; natural log is conventional.']],
String.raw`Suppose the model gives each of three observed outcomes probability $1/10$. Their joint likelihood is $(1/10)^3=1/1000$. With base-10 logs for easy arithmetic, each example contributes $-\log_{10}(1/10)=1$ to the loss, so the total is $1+1+1=3$. With hundreds of examples like these, the product can round to zero while the sum stays easy to represent.`,
String.raw`Training uses derivatives to see how changing a weight changes the score. For a product of likelihoods, the product rule says to differentiate one factor at a time and multiply by all the other factors. With a sum of log-likelihoods, you differentiate each example’s term on its own and add the results.`),
F('Gaussian regression negative log-likelihood',String.raw`y_n\mid x_n,\theta\sim\mathcal N(x_n^T\theta,\sigma^2),\qquad\mathcal L(\theta)=\frac1{2\sigma^2}\sum_n(y_n-x_n^T\theta)^2+\frac N2\log(2\pi\sigma^2)`, 'Slides 8 and 12. Assumes fixed positive residual variance σ².',
'This connects a probabilistic assumption to the familiar squared-error loss.', 'Model the target as a linear mean plus Gaussian residual noise; its negative log-likelihood is scaled squared error plus a normalization term.', [[String.raw`$x_n^T\theta$`,'A dot product giving the predicted mean: multiply corresponding features and weights, then add.'],[String.raw`$\sigma^2>0$`,'Variance of residual noise around that mean.'],[String.raw`$\mathcal N(\mu,\sigma^2)$`,'A normal distribution with mean μ and variance σ².'],['$N$','Number of observations.']],
String.raw`The two actual rents are 11 and 9, in thousands of pesos. Model A predicts 10 and 10, so its squared errors add to $1^2+(-1)^2=2$. Model B predicts 11 and 7, so its squared errors add to $0^2+2^2=4$. For the same fixed variance, A has the lower negative log-likelihood and therefore the higher likelihood.`, 'For fixed variance, minimizing this objective over the weights gives the same minimizers as squared error. If variance is also optimized, its normalization term cannot be discarded.')],
steps:['State the model and independence assumptions. “Identically distributed” means a shared data-generating distribution; independence allows the product factorization.','Use log(ab) = log(a) + log(b), then negate to switch maximization to minimization.','When dropping a term, explain why it is constant with respect to the variables currently optimized.'],caveat:'The deck’s Gaussian likelihood is a model of target residual noise. It should not be confused with choosing a Gaussian prior over the weights. MLE by itself does not add a prior or a weight penalty.',check:'Does taking a log change the parameter value that maximizes a positive likelihood?',answer:'No. Log is strictly increasing, so it preserves maximizers. Negating the log turns the same solution into a minimizer.',figure:12,caption:'The Gaussian likelihood expansion separates the squared residual term from the normalization term.'},
{title:'A prior becomes a regularizer',short:'MAP and Gaussian priors',slides:[13,14],time:'12 min',lead:'MAP combines evidence from the data with a preference over parameter values.',
idea:'A prior describes parameter preferences before conditioning on this dataset. Bayes’ rule combines prior and likelihood into a posterior. MAP chooses a parameter setting at the peak of that posterior. In negative-log form, the likelihood supplies a data-fitting loss and the prior supplies a penalty. A zero-mean isotropic Gaussian prior produces L2 shrinkage; a more general covariance produces a direction-dependent quadratic penalty.',
formulas:[F('Posterior and MAP objective',String.raw`p(\theta\mid\mathcal D)=\frac{p(\mathcal D\mid\theta)p(\theta)}{p(\mathcal D)},\qquad\theta_{\rm MAP}=\arg\min_\theta[-\log p(\mathcal D\mid\theta)-\log p(\theta)]`, 'Slides 13–14. The log-posterior equality includes a constant −log p(D).',
'Among settings that explain the data, MAP can prefer ones favored by a prior.', 'Multiply likelihood by prior and normalize. To find the posterior peak, minimize negative log-likelihood plus negative log-prior.', [[String.raw`$\mathcal D$`,'The observed dataset, held fixed in this optimization.'],[String.raw`$p(\theta)$`,'Prior over parameters.'],[String.raw`$p(\mathcal D\mid\theta)$ and $p(\theta\mid\mathcal D)$`,'Likelihood of the data and posterior over parameters. Conditioning is reversed.'],[String.raw`$p(\mathcal D)$ and $\arg\min$`,'The evidence, constant with respect to θ; argmin returns the minimizing parameter value, not the objective’s value.']],
'Suppose A uses a large near-campus weight. Its likelihood is $1/2$ and its prior score is $1/4$, so its posterior score before rescaling is $1/8$. B uses a smaller weight. Its likelihood is $1/4$ and its prior score is 1, so its score before rescaling is $1/4$. MLE prefers A, while MAP prefers B.', 'The evidence can be ignored for comparing θ within the same model and fixed data. That does not make it irrelevant for every Bayesian problem.'),
F('Gaussian prior gives a quadratic penalty',String.raw`-\log p(\theta)=\tfrac12\theta^T\Sigma^{-1}\theta+C,\qquad\Sigma=\tau^2I\ \Rightarrow\ -\log p(\theta)=\frac{\|\theta\|_2^2}{2\tau^2}+C`, 'Clarifies slide 14: a plain λθᵀθ penalty assumes isotropic covariance, up to constants.',
'The shape and spread of the prior determine which parameter values are expensive.', 'A Gaussian prior charges according to squared distance from zero measured using its inverse covariance; an isotropic prior charges the ordinary sum of squares.', [[String.raw`$\Sigma$`,'Positive-definite covariance matrix describing prior spread and dependencies.'],[String.raw`$\tau^2 I$`,'Isotropic covariance: the same variance τ² in every direction, with identity matrix I.'],[String.raw`$\lambda=1/(2\tau^2)$`,'Penalty coefficient before any rescaling of the data loss.'],['$C$','A term constant with respect to θ when the prior covariance is fixed.']],
String.raw`If $\tau^2=1/2$, then $\lambda=1$. A weight vector $(1,-2)$ has negative log-prior 5 plus a constant.`, 'A narrower prior means a larger shrinkage coefficient. A generic Gaussian covariance does not always reduce to one scalar times θᵀθ.')],
steps:['Distinguish likelihood, prior and posterior by reading which quantity is conditioned on which.','Apply negative log to the likelihood–prior product.','State the prior assumptions before identifying the corresponding penalty.'],caveat:'MAP selects a point estimate, not the whole posterior distribution. A prior that is constant over the relevant parameter region makes MAP and MLE agree there; an improper uniform prior needs separate care.',check:'Does a Gaussian likelihood automatically imply L2 regularization?',answer:'No. A fixed-variance Gaussian residual likelihood gives a squared data-fitting loss. A Gaussian parameter prior gives a quadratic parameter penalty.',figure:14,caption:'The red term fits the data; the blue term expresses the prior preference over parameters.'},
{title:'Create useful variations of the data',short:'Label-preserving augmentation',slides:[20,21,22,23,24,25],time:'9 min',lead:'Augmentation teaches which changes should leave the answer unchanged—and which should not.',
idea:'Data augmentation creates modified training inputs so the model sees plausible variations beyond the original finite sample. A transformation is useful only when its target remains correct or is transformed appropriately. Flipping a dog photograph may preserve its class; flipping a letter can change its identity. Scaling, translation and mild distortion encode assumptions about what variation matters. STRAug targets scene-text distortions such as geometry, blur and imaging effects.',
formulas:[],steps:['Identify the target and the real variations expected at evaluation time.','Check whether each proposed transform preserves the label. For detection or segmentation, transform boxes and masks along with the image.','Fit preprocessing statistics on training data only; apply the chosen procedure consistently to validation and test inputs.'],caveat:'Augmented copies are correlated examples, not independent new observations. Normalization and ZCA whitening are listed among preprocessing operations on slide 22; they do not necessarily create additional examples or preserve labels under arbitrary use. The STRAug result slides illustrate specific experiments, not guaranteed gains on every dataset.',check:'Why might a horizontal flip help animal classification but harm character recognition?',answer:'The animal’s class often survives the flip, while a character’s identity can change. The valid invariance is determined by the task.',figure:21,caption:'The character example demonstrates why a visually simple transform can invalidate the original label.'},
{title:'Erase, blend, or replace a patch',short:'CutOut, MixUp and CutMix',slides:[26,27,28,29],time:'13 min',lead:'The way you change the input determines what must happen to the target.',
idea:'CutOut removes a region from one image, encouraging the model to use other evidence while typically retaining the original class target. MixUp blends two inputs and blends their targets by the same coefficient. CutMix replaces a spatial patch with content from another image; its target weights reflect the area kept from each source. A one-hot class target is a vector with 1 at the class position and 0 elsewhere; mixing produces a soft target rather than one hard class.',
formulas:[F('MixUp input and target',String.raw`\tilde x=\lambda x_1+(1-\lambda)x_2,\qquad\tilde y=\lambda y_1+(1-\lambda)y_2`, 'Slide 28. λ is a mixing weight here, not the weight-penalty coefficient.',
'The training example becomes a controlled blend with a matching target.', 'Take lambda of example 1 and one minus lambda of example 2, for both the input and the label vector.', [[String.raw`$\lambda\in[0,1]$`,'Fraction of the first input in the blend.'],['$x_1,x_2$','Two compatible input arrays, combined entry by entry.'],['$y_1,y_2$','One-hot or soft target vectors in the same class order.'],[String.raw`$\tilde x,\tilde y$`,'The new blended input and its soft target.']],
String.raw`For bird and cat targets $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$ with $\lambda=3/4$, the target becomes $(3/4,1/4)$.`, 'Do not keep the label purely “bird” just because bird has the larger mixing weight.'),
F('CutMix mask and area-weighted target',String.raw`\tilde x=M\odot x_1+(1-M)\odot x_2,\qquad\lambda=\frac{\sum M}{HW},\qquad\tilde y=\lambda y_1+(1-\lambda)y_2`, 'Slide 29, with the area fraction made explicit. M = 1 means keep source 1.',
'A pasted patch changes the source content by spatial area, not by blending every pixel.', 'Use the binary mask to choose the source at each location; weight the label by the fraction retained from each image.', [['$M$ and $1-M$','Complementary binary spatial masks, broadcast across channels.'],[String.raw`$\odot$`,'Entrywise multiplication, selecting which pixels survive.'],['$H,W$','Image height and width; HW is the total spatial area.'],[String.raw`$\lambda$`,'Actual fraction of image area from source 1 after any boundary clipping.']],
String.raw`A 4-by-4 image receives a 2-by-2 patch from source 2. Source 1 keeps 12 of 16 pixels, so $\lambda=3/4$ and bird/cat targets mix as $(3/4,1/4)$.`, 'Unlike CutOut, CutMix inserts another labeled image and mixes targets. Patch area is a training rule, not an exact measurement of visible semantic content.')],
steps:['State whether pixels are erased, globally blended, or spatially replaced.','Write the class-vector order before mixing labels.','For CutMix, count the actual retained area and make sure the weights add to 1.'],caveat:'CutOut’s unchanged target assumes enough label-relevant evidence remains; excessive occlusion can make a task impossible. MixUp and CutMix targets are intentionally soft training targets, not claims that the image literally has fractional objects.',check:'What is the target difference between CutOut and CutMix?',answer:'CutOut typically retains the original target. CutMix mixes the two source targets according to the area contributed by each image.',figure:29,caption:'The mask selects spatial regions; the target uses an area fraction.'},
{title:'Make views agree; choose a policy',short:'AgMax and RandAugment',slides:[30,31,32],time:'8 min',lead:'Choosing variations and encouraging consistent predictions are related but distinct decisions.',
idea:'AgMax encourages agreement between representations or predictions from two augmented views of the same input, alongside a supervised learning signal. It promotes stability to useful transformations. RandAugment is a policy for selecting and composing augmentation operations with a reduced search space. It controls how views are made; agreement regularization controls how the model responds to those views.',
formulas:[],steps:['Check that both views should convey the same target.','Keep a supervised or other informative learning signal so agreement does not reward a useless constant answer.','Distinguish choosing augmentation operations from adding an objective that encourages agreement.'],caveat:'Slide 30 gives the agreement idea but not the full AgMax objective. A simple squared difference between predictions can illustrate consistency, but is not the paper’s exact information-theoretic objective. Slide 31 compares particular ImageNet experiments; read its results within that setup.',check:'Would two identical predictions be sufficient evidence that a model is useful?',answer:'No. A model that outputs the same class for every input can agree with itself while being inaccurate. Agreement must be paired with a useful task-learning signal.',figure:30,caption:'Two augmented views share an underlying example; their learned outputs are encouraged to agree.'},
{title:'Practice under small perturbations',short:'Noise and domain randomization',slides:[33,34,35,36,37,38],time:'10 min',lead:'If small irrelevant changes destroy a prediction, teach the model to tolerate those changes during training.',
idea:'Input noise changes the observed input while retaining a valid target, encouraging robustness to that kind of perturbation. Weight noise perturbs the parameters so a useful solution must tolerate nearby weight settings. Domain randomization varies simulation conditions such as texture, lighting or physical properties to reduce dependence on one synthetic environment. The deck’s demonstrations illustrate this broad sim-to-real motivation; slide 37 is a dataset example, not a new regularization formula.',
formulas:[F('Input and weight perturbations · supplementary notation',String.raw`\tilde x=x+\eta_x,\qquad\tilde\theta=\theta+\eta_\theta`, 'Formalizes slides 33 and 38; the noise distribution and magnitude must be specified by the task.',
'Perturbed training exposes the model to a neighborhood of inputs or parameters rather than one exact setting.', 'Add an input-noise sample to x, or a weight-noise sample to theta, before the relevant forward computation.', [[String.raw`$x,\theta$`,'Original input and parameter vector.'],[String.raw`$\eta_x,\eta_\theta$`,'Noise samples, possibly drawn from a zero-mean distribution.'],[String.raw`$\tilde x,\tilde\theta$`,'Perturbed input or weights. These are different interventions.']],
'If an input value is 2 and the sampled noise is −1/10, the perturbed value is $19/10$. Whether the target stays correct depends on the task and perturbation size.', 'Training should minimize an objective averaged over the perturbations, or approximate it through sampling. Simply adding arbitrary noise does not automatically create a useful regularizer.')],
steps:['Identify the nuisance variation you want the model to tolerate.','Choose a plausible perturbation size and check that targets remain valid.','Evaluate robustness on held-out conditions, including conditions not identical to training perturbations.'],caveat:'The normal-equation expressions on slide 38 are illustrative algebra, not a general algorithm for training deep networks with noisy weights. They require linear-model and invertibility assumptions; do not memorize “optimal weights minus noise” as the training rule. Noise encourages local insensitivity under suitable assumptions, not immunity to every corruption.',check:'Why might randomizing simulation textures help a real-world robot?',answer:'It reduces the usefulness of relying on one simulator’s appearance, encouraging features that remain useful across varied environments. Transfer still depends on whether important real conditions are represented.',figure:34,caption:'Varied simulation conditions support the goal of transferring learned behavior to a real setting.'},
{title:'Learn related tasks together',short:'Multi-task learning',slides:[39],time:'8 min',lead:'A shared representation must support several related tasks, which can reduce reliance on one task’s accidental shortcuts.',
idea:'Multi-task learning uses shared parameters for several objectives, often with a shared backbone and separate task heads. For example, an image representation may serve classification, bounding-box prediction and segmentation. Sharing acts as a soft constraint: parameters useful to one task must also work for the others. Each head may still keep task-specific parameters.',
formulas:[F('Weighted multi-task objective · supplementary notation',String.raw`J(\phi,\psi_1,\ldots,\psi_K)=\sum_{k=1}^{K}a_k L_k(\phi,\psi_k)`, 'Formalizes the shared-backbone idea on slide 39; a specific weighting rule is not given in the deck.',
'We need one training objective that reflects multiple tasks while allowing a common representation.', 'Add the task losses after weighting them; each task depends on shared backbone parameters and its own head.', [[String.raw`$\phi$`,'Parameters shared across tasks.'],[String.raw`$\psi_k$`,'Parameters specific to task k.'],['$L_k$ and $a_k$','Task k’s loss and its nonnegative relative weight.'],['$K$','Number of tasks.']],
'For classification loss 2, box loss 4, and weights 1 and 1/2, the joint objective is $2+(1/2)4=4$.', 'Tasks with different numerical loss scales may need balancing. Conflicting tasks can cause negative transfer rather than helpful regularization.')],
steps:['Identify exactly which parameters are shared and which are task-specific.','Explain what common structure makes the tasks related.','Check task balance and per-task validation performance, not only the total loss.'],caveat:'Sharing is beneficial only when the tasks provide compatible information. More tasks are not automatically better; gradients from one task can interfere with another.',check:'Why is multi-task learning described as a soft constraint?',answer:'The shared weights are influenced by several task losses, which discourages solutions that fit only one task’s idiosyncrasies without explicitly forbidding every such solution.',figure:39,caption:'The shared backbone supports different task heads; each head has its own target.'},
{title:'Reuse the same parameters across inputs',short:'Parameter sharing and Siamese nets',slides:[40],time:'7 min',lead:'Two branches can process different inputs using one learned function.',
idea:'In a Siamese design, two branches apply the same parameterized encoder to different inputs. They do not merely have similar-looking architectures: their weights are tied. This reduces the number of independently adjustable parameters and forces the same representation rule to work across both inputs. The deck illustrates this with left and right images for disparity estimation.',
formulas:[F('Shared encoder · supplementary notation',String.raw`h_1=f(x_1;\theta),\qquad h_2=f(x_2;\theta)`, 'Formalizes slide 40. The same θ is used in both branches.',
'An architecture can encode a relationship between inputs instead of learning two unrelated encoders.', 'Run input 1 and input 2 through the same function with the same weights, producing two representations.', [['$x_1,x_2$','Different inputs such as left and right camera images.'],[String.raw`$f(\cdot;\theta)$`,'One encoder with tied parameters.'],['$h_1,h_2$','Representations that can differ because the inputs differ.']],
'If one encoder has 10 trainable parameters, two independently weighted copies have 20. Tying the encoder weights keeps 10 distinct encoder parameters, excluding any separate comparison head.', 'Shared parameters do not imply identical outputs. The same function can produce different representations for different inputs.')],
steps:['Count distinct trainable parameters, not the number of times a function is used.','Separate weight sharing from merely copying an architecture.','Explain why the task should benefit from a common feature-extraction rule.'],caveat:'Related input distributions motivate sharing, but do not prove that every representation should be identical. Additional heads may have separate parameters, and sharing does not necessarily halve computation.',check:'If two branches have equal architecture but independently trained weights, are their parameters shared?',answer:'No. Sharing requires tying the actual parameter values during training; architecture equality alone is insufficient.',figure:40,caption:'Left and right image branches share encoder parameters before their representations are compared.'},
{title:'Stop at the useful checkpoint',short:'Early stopping',slides:[41],time:'8 min',lead:'More training can improve memorization while making unseen-data performance worse.',
idea:'Early stopping monitors a validation metric during training and selects a checkpoint before overfitting becomes worse. It limits how far the training procedure moves through parameter space. In practice, patience tolerates several non-improving checks because validation curves can fluctuate. Save the best checkpoint and restore it rather than automatically using the final weights.',
formulas:[F('Select the best validation checkpoint',String.raw`t^*=\arg\min_t L_{\mathrm{val}}(\theta_t),\qquad\theta_{\mathrm{selected}}=\theta_{t^*}`, 'Formalizes slide 41. This version assumes a loss to minimize.',
'We need a principled checkpoint-selection rule based on unseen development data.', 'Choose the saved training step with the lowest validation loss, then use the parameters saved at that step.', [[String.raw`$t$ and $\theta_t$`,'A monitored training step or epoch and its saved parameters.'],[String.raw`$L_{\mathrm{val}}$`,'Loss evaluated on held-out validation data.'],[String.raw`$t^*$ and $\arg\min$`,'The best monitored step; argmin returns its index.']],
'Validation losses across epochs 1–4 are $5,3,2,4$. Epoch 3 has the best loss, so restore its parameters even if training stops at epoch 4.', 'For a metric such as accuracy to maximize, reverse the selection direction. Patience determines when to stop checking, not which checkpoint is best.')],
steps:['Choose a validation metric and whether smaller or larger is better.','Save improvements; count non-improving checks using a stated patience and minimum-improvement rule.','Stop when the rule triggers and restore the best saved parameters.'],caveat:'The simplest slide explanation stops when validation error first rises. Patience is a practical extension. Early stopping does not use the test set, and a noisy validation increase is not by itself proof of overfitting.',check:'Why restore the best checkpoint instead of the checkpoint that triggered stopping?',answer:'The stopping checkpoint can have worse validation performance; patience deliberately allows training past the best observed point.',figure:41,caption:'The validation curve can worsen while the training curve continues to improve.'},
{title:'Train with missing units',short:'Dropout and keep probability',slides:[42,43],time:'11 min',lead:'Dropout discourages the network from depending on one fixed combination of hidden units.',
idea:'During training, dropout randomly masks activations, producing different thinned networks across passes. Units must remain useful when some other units are absent, reducing fragile co-adaptation. During ordinary evaluation, keep all units and use a consistent scaling convention. In this deck, p means the probability of keeping a unit; some software APIs instead use p to mean the probability of dropping it.',
formulas:[F('Inverted dropout · supplementary scaling detail',String.raw`m_j\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p),\qquad\tilde h_j=\frac{m_jh_j}{p},\qquad\mathbb E[\tilde h_j\mid h_j]=h_j`, 'Slide 43 defines p as keep probability. Inverted scaling is an implementation clarification, not an equation shown in the deck.',
'Random masking should not unintentionally shrink the average activation scale.', 'Keep activation j with probability p and scale surviving activations by 1/p; their expected value matches the original activation.', [['$h_j$','The original activation of unit j.'],[String.raw`$m_j\in\{0,1\}$`,'Random mask: 1 keeps the unit and 0 masks it.'],[String.raw`$0<p\le1$`,'Keep probability, not drop probability in this notation.'],[String.raw`$\mathbb E$ and $\tilde h_j$`,'Expected value over masks and the masked/scaled training activation.']],
'With $h=4$ and $p=1/2$, the training activation is 8 if kept and 0 if dropped. Its expectation is $(1/2)8+(1/2)0=4$.', 'With inverted dropout, evaluation uses the unmasked activation without an extra scaling step. Under the original convention, training masks without dividing by p and evaluation applies matching scaling. Do not combine both conventions.')],
steps:['State whether p denotes keep or drop probability.','Specify the training scaling convention before computing a masked activation.','At evaluation, disable ordinary random masking and apply only the scaling appropriate to that convention.'],caveat:'Matching expected activations is not an exact equality between a nonlinear network’s output and the average output of all its thinned networks. Excessive dropout can underfit. Monte Carlo dropout is a separate inference procedure, beyond the deck’s ordinary evaluation description.',check:'An API uses dropout rate 0.2. What is the keep probability for our equation?',answer:'The keep probability is 0.8. Confusing the drop rate with the keep probability changes both the masking frequency and the scaling.',figure:43,caption:'The deck’s p is the training keep probability; at test time the unit is included.'},
{title:'Validate choices and combine the tools',short:'Cross-validation and exam synthesis',slides:[44,45,46],time:'11 min',lead:'Regularization choices need evidence from data that did not train the model being evaluated.',
idea:'K-fold cross-validation partitions development data into K folds. Each fold is used once for validation while the other folds train a fresh model; the validation results are averaged. This uses limited data more thoroughly for selection, at the cost of repeated training. The final test set remains separate. The deck closes with a toolbox: penalties, augmentation, noise, sharing and dropout address different sources of overfitting.',
formulas:[F('K-fold validation average',String.raw`\overline s=\frac1K\sum_{k=1}^{K}s_k`, 'Slide 44. Each sk must come from a model trained without validation fold k.',
'A single small holdout split can give a noisy picture. Multiple held-out folds provide several development evaluations.', 'Add the K held-out fold scores and divide by K.', [['$K$','Number of folds and model fits for one candidate configuration.'],['$s_k$','Validation score on fold k from that fold’s freshly trained model.'],[String.raw`$\overline s$`,'Mean score. Keep the same metric and direction across folds.']],
'For four equal-sized folds with accuracy $3/4,1/2,3/4,1$, the mean is $3/4$. With 100 examples, each fit trains on 75 and validates on 25.', 'Fit preprocessing and feature-selection steps inside each training fold. Related samples need group-aware splitting; temporal tasks need time-aware evaluation rather than arbitrary shuffling.')],
steps:['Choose a split strategy that respects subjects, groups or time dependence.','Reinitialize and fit the whole training pipeline separately for each fold; compare candidates by validation results.','Once choices are fixed, train the final model using the chosen procedure and evaluate once on untouched test data.'],caveat:'Four-fold validation means four fits per candidate, not four successive phases of training the same weights. The deck’s small-dataset size range is a use-case example, not a strict cutoff. Training folds overlap, so fold scores are not independent replicates.',check:'Why is fitting normalization on the entire dataset before cross-validation a problem?',answer:'Validation folds would influence preprocessing used during training. Fit normalization inside each training fold to avoid that information leak.',figure:44,caption:'Each row is a separate training run with a different held-out fold.'}
];
